A||N||10||fropuff||Breaking into car; keys locked in (Grand Am)||||8||
Z||000000||fropuff||12-08-2001||11:11 AM||||Hey guys.  I accidentally locked the keys in my cousins smashed up early-90s Grand Am (around a 91).  OOPS.  If has power locks, so I don't think a slim jim would work.  However, the hood is popped.  I was thinking that I saw on a movie one time when a criminal (not me) shorted the wires for the power locks and the locks popped up.  I want to try something like that.  Or, if there's any other way, besides calling the locksmith, please let me know.  It has a sunroof, but the latch is strong.  I already tried that.  I think he would notice a broken window, because his glass is all okay.  Thanks a lot for any help.||||reg||8
Z||000001||maryjane||12-08-2001||11:20 AM||||Why not just tell your cousin? He may have an extra set of keys. I've seen thread combustion occur over this question being posted before, as it might give inept auto thieves more insight to their trade.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||12-08-2001||11:24 AM||||I'm sorry.  I forgot the most important part: if you have an idea, you can PM me or email me.   <A HREF="mailto:FroDaddy247@aol.com">FroDaddy247@aol.com</A>   As for having another key, he doesn't.  It's a big mess.  He got into an accident last summer, his fault, no insurance, no DL.  The cops towed it.  My parents bought it back and are paying on it; it is sitting in our yard with a smashed up front end.  If I have to call the locksmith, I will, because it is my fault.  I just wanted to try other options as well.  Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TSPalmer||12-08-2001||11:35 AM||||Unless I'm wrong (which does happen on occasion), you should be able to use a slim jim. Either that or the ole coat hanger trick...<P>Good Luck<P>------------------<BR>T.Shawn Palmer<BR>'85 SE 4cyl 5spd||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||12-08-2001||11:44 AM||||Welll, I can't remember who said it, but someone in the chat told me that Grand Ams have a weird locking setup.  He said that if a Slim Jim was used, it would screw up the locking mechanism.  But he said he wasn't sure.  Thanks.<P>BTW: If you have any idea, PLEASE don't post it here.  I know everyone knows the Slim Jim, so it's not bad.  But, I just don't want to be the starter of another flaming O/T thread.  Thanks again.  I appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||12-08-2001||11:45 AM||||power locks wont affect using a slim jim or similar. maybe even pry the door far enough to get coat hanger in to trip the lock switch. (i have)||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||12-08-2001||11:55 AM||||I don't understand why people are so worried about telling you how to break into your own car.  I remember the flame war that went on from the last one.  but here is the thing.... this is the internet people.  I actually looked up lockpicking yesterday.  And you know what?  there are like six million places telling you how to do it.  So who cares?<P>------------------<BR>Kills:<BR>97 Neon R/T<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>         <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||12-08-2001||12:14 PM||||Grand Ams have framed windows dont they? That makes things harder. On most fieros you can pull the window back almost half an inch or more. That makes things pretty easy...<P>If all else fails you can smash the window. Its a junker from what you have said. You can get a new window for cheap.<P><BR>Oh, and I allways think of this funny way to break into a fiero. Take a hot sodering iron or something simmilar. Then melt a big hole in the roof with it, jump through the headliner, and your in!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Or melt hole in door panel and unlock it that way. lol||||reg||1
Z||000008||TSPalmer||12-08-2001||01:00 PM||||Sushi, you da man!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||fierohoho||12-08-2001||01:58 PM||||Take a clothes pin and pinch it so it froms a wedge.<P>Push the wedge between the glass and dew wipe about 8-10" from the back edge of the door glass so you have a gap that allows you to look down inside the door with a small flashlight.<P>The gap may only be about a 1/4" wide<P>I would do this on the passenger side so in the off chance you screw up the lock you won't have to crawl across.<P>Look in the door and you should see two rods going down from the handle and lock, if you pull the handle you can see this one move.<P>Slide the slim jim down the "lock rod" until it bottoms out, apply downward presure as you or a friend watch the lock button, the pressure need is fairly hard but you should see the button start to move, if it moves a little (it does not need to go all the way for the door to unlock) try the handle.<P>I find this works for most GM cars with or without power locks.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||JSocha||12-08-2001||05:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>I actually looked up lockpicking yesterday.  And you know what?  there are like six million places telling you how to do it.  So who cares?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Maybe nobody wants Cliff's site to become six million and "one" places telling one how to do it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||10
